// NEGATIVE CONTROL for the directive-conflict rule. The runner must report FAIL
// for this file; the self-test block inverts the verdict.
//
// `// EXPECT:` asserts a program's OUTPUT, which presupposes it ran. The
// rejection directives assert it never got that far. A file carrying both is
// incoherent, and the runner rejects the combination rather than silently
// honouring one of them.
//
// Without this control the conflict rule is the one rule in the runner with no
// coverage, so it could quietly become a no-op — at which point a test carrying
// both directives would be scored on whichever branch happened to run first, and
// a contributor who added `// EXPECT:` to a rejection test would get a green
// result for a test that asserts nothing.
// EXPECT: ok
// EXPECT_FAIL:
println("ok")
